  submodularity violations: 0/119658;  lattice failures: 0/300;  first-order set outside an optimum: 0/300

=== (2) accretion in upkeep h (Topkis), same model family ===
  largest optimal set lost a level as h fell: 0/2320 steps

=== (3) accretion in STAKES without fallback (power class, all costs scale s^a): check ===
  level lost as stakes rose (no fallback): 0/1440

=== (4) COUNTEREXAMPLE: bounded lookup fallback breaks stakes-accretion and lookup-cost monotonicity ===
  stakes s=1, lookup cost mu*u=2: flat=148.32 level=146.39 -> LEVEL
  stakes s=4, lookup cost mu*u=2: flat=200.00 level=209.44 -> no level
  stakes s=1, lookup cost mu*u=1.4: flat=140.00 level=144.97 -> no level
  stakes s=1, lookup cost mu*u=9e+09: flat=148.32 level=146.39 -> LEVEL
  stakes s=4, lookup cost mu*u=9e+09: flat=296.65 level=292.29 -> LEVEL
  => with a fallback, raising stakes (1->4) or cheapening lookup (2->1.4) DISSOLVES the level; without one it stays.

=== (5) COUNTEREXAMPLE: convex member cost (read-rate cap + quartic loss) -> levels are substitutes ===
  J: {(): np.float64(520.0), (0,): np.float64(432.0), (1,): np.float64(432.0), (0, 1): np.float64(504.0)}
  submodularity violations 2/6; lattice holds: False; optima [(0,), (1,)]
  marginal member saving of level 2: alone = 320.0  given level 1 = 160.0
  each level pays alone (first-order/standalone rule keeps both), but the optima are {0} or {1}: union not optimal, inclusion fails.

